1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,280
The person I've chosen to talk about is the French writer Victor Hugo

2
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:08,900
many people have heard of him

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:13,480
because his novel, Les Misérables, which he wrote in 1862

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:16,020
is famous around the world.

5
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:20,020
It became a stage musical in the 1980s

6
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:24,170
and a film version was also released in 2012.

7
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:29,320
So, some of us, I'm sure, have a pretty general idea of the plot

8
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,670
but we know much less about the author.

9
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:37,671
Today, I'm going to provide a little more insight into this talented man

10
00:00:37,671 --> 00:00:45,530
and I'm going to talk particularly about the home he had on the island of Guernsey in the British Channel Islands.

11
00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:50,539
But first, his early career... as I've said, he was a writer,

12
00:00:50,539 --> 00:00:54,360
he was at the height of his career in Paris

13
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:57,860
and he was very highly regarded by his colleagues.

14
00:00:57,860 --> 00:01:00,580
As far as literature was concerned

15
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:03,990
he was the leading figure of the Romantic movement.

16
00:01:03,990 --> 00:01:07,496
However, as well as being a literary genius,

17
00:01:07,496 --> 00:01:14,070
he also gave many speeches about issues like the level of poverty in his society.

18
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:19,000
He felt very strongly about this and about other areas

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,560
where change was needed, like education.

20
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:31,244
This kind of outspoken criticism was not well liked by the rulers of France and, eventually, the emperor -

21
00:01:31,244 --> 00:01:36,780
Napoleon III - told Victor Hugo to leave Paris and not return

22
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:40,410
in other words, he sent him into exile.

23
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:46,270
So Victor Hugo was forced to reside in other parts of Europe.

24
00:01:46,270 --> 00:01:53,410
Guernsey was actually his third place of exile and he landed there in 1855.

25
00:01:53,410 --> 00:01:57,494
He produced a lot while on Guernsey - including Les Misérables -

26
00:01:57,494 --> 00:02:04,920
and to do this, he had to spend a great deal of time in the home that he had there.

27
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:12,840
This was a property that he bought using the money he'd made in France from the publication of a collection of his poetry.

28
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:22,350
It was the only property he ever owned, and he was very proud of it.

29
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:26,980
The property Victor Hugo bought on Guernsey was a large,

30
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:34,850
five-storey house in the capital town of St Peter Port and he lived there for 15 years,

31
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:39,480
returning to France in 1870 when Napoleon's Empire collapsed.

32
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:43,320
He decorated and furnished each level, or floor

33
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:46,148
of the house in unique and wonderful ways,

34
00:02:46,148 --> 00:02:52,400
and many people consider the inside of the house to be a 'work of art'.

35
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:59,930
Today it's a museum that attracts 200,000 visitors a year.

36
00:02:59,930 --> 00:03:02,200
He lived in the house with his family

37
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:07,933
and portraits of its members still hang in rooms on the ground floor,

38
00:03:07,933 --> 00:03:14,030
along with drawings that he did during his travels that he felt were important to him.

39
00:03:14,030 --> 00:03:21,490
In other ground-floor rooms, there are huge tapestries that he would have designed and loved.

40
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:30,760
The walls are covered in dark wood panelling that Victor Hugo created himself using wooden furniture that he bought in the market.

41
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:34,020
The items were relatively inexpensive

42
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:38,020
and he used them to create intricate carvings.

43
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:44,370
They gave an atmosphere on the lower level that was shadowy and rather solemn.

44
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:49,680
On the next level of the house there are two impressive lounges

45
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:52,200
where he entertained his guests.

46
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:57,080
One lounge has entirely red furnishings

47
00:03:57,080 --> 00:04:01,900
such as sofas and wall coverings, and the other blue.

48
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:10,469
There's a strong Chinese influence in these areas in things like the wallpaper pattern and the lamps -

49
00:04:10,469 --> 00:04:15,230
which he would have made himself by copying original versions.

50
00:04:15,230 --> 00:04:19,900
His library, where he left many of his favourite books

51
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:22,585
forms the hallway to the third floor

52
00:04:22,585 --> 00:04:28,210
and was a comfortable area where he could relax and enjoy his afternoons.

53
00:04:28,210 --> 00:04:31,716
And then, at the very top of the house,

54
00:04:31,716 --> 00:04:33,980
there's a room called the Lookout

55
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:37,950
called that because it looks out over the harbour.

56
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:41,310
In contrast to the rather dark lower levels,

57
00:04:41,310 --> 00:04:46,413
it's full of light and was like a glass office

58
00:04:46,453 --> 00:04:50,130
where he would write until lunchtime - often at his desk

59
00:04:50,130 --> 00:04:58,000
So, Victor Hugo was a man of many talents, but he was also true to his values.

60
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,389
While living in his house on Guernsey, he entertained many other famous writers,

61
00:05:03,389 --> 00:05:12,510
but he also invited a large group of local children from the deprived areas of the island to dinner once a week.

62
00:05:12,510 --> 00:05:15,980
What's more, he served them their food

63
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:20,240
which was an extraordinary gesture for the time period.

64
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:25,557
In 1927, the house was owned by his relatives,

65
00:05:26,279 --> 00:05:29,710
and they decided to donate it to the city of Paris

66
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:35,091
It has since been restored using photographs from the period and,

67
00:05:35,091 --> 00:05:41,940
as I mentioned earlier, is now a museum that is open to the public.
